+# Serenox Go Rewrite — Agent Prompt
+
+You are the implementation agent responsible for rewriting the existing Python Telegram userbot in Go.
+
+## Workspace Rules
+
+- Treat the current repository root as the read-only legacy reference.
+- Read and analyze the existing Python source, especially `hikka/main.py`, `hikka/client.py`, `hikka/dispatcher.py`, `hikka/loader.py`, `hikka/types.py`, `hikka/database.py`, `hikka/inline/`, and `hikka/modules/`.
+- Create all new implementation files only inside a new top-level directory named `serenox-go/`.
+- Do not edit, move, rename, overwrite, or delete any existing legacy file or directory.
+- Do not perform a destructive migration. The Python implementation must remain runnable throughout the rewrite.
+- Work incrementally. Keep the new project buildable and tested after every completed milestone.
+
+## Objective
+
+Build a production-ready, low-memory, high-throughput Go replacement that preserves the important behavior of the legacy userbot while providing:
+
+1. a native Go core;
+2. built-in Go modules;
+3. external Go plugins through a versioned process/RPC protocol;
+4. an optional isolated Python sidecar for legacy Python plugins;
+5. bounded concurrency, backpressure, clean shutdown, and observable resource usage.
+
+Do not embed CPython into the Go process. Run Python compatibility code as a supervised child process and communicate over a local Unix socket, with a portable transport abstraction for other operating systems.
+
+## Required Architecture
+
+Create clear packages for:
+
+- Telegram session and update transport;
+- command parsing and dispatch;
+- watcher filtering and dispatch;
+- bounded per-plugin queues and worker limits;
+- rate limiting without one timer per event;
+- configuration and persistent storage;
+- inline bot support;
+- module registry and lifecycle;
+- plugin RPC protocol and process supervision;
+- structured logging, metrics, graceful shutdown, and recovery;
+- Python compatibility host and SDK.
+
+Apply filters before sending updates to plugins. Never create an unbounded goroutine, task, queue, cache, or timer collection. Every background worker must have ownership, cancellation, shutdown, and test coverage.
+
+Use explicit, versioned DTOs for messages, updates, commands, actions, configuration values, errors, and plugin metadata. Keep Telegram-library-specific types out of the public plugin protocol.
+
+## Plugin Model
+
+Support these plugin categories:
+
+- built-in Go modules compiled into the core;
+- external Go executables using the versioned RPC protocol;
+- legacy Python modules hosted by the optional Python sidecar.
+
+Implement a small stable Python compatibility package for the commonly used Hikka APIs, including module lifecycle hooks, command and watcher decorators, configuration, storage, replies/edits, inline actions, and client operations. Do not claim full legacy compatibility until verified by automated compatibility fixtures. Unsupported legacy APIs must fail with explicit actionable errors.
+
+## Migration Order
+
+1. Inventory the legacy behavior and write an architecture decision record.
+2. Scaffold `serenox-go/` without modifying the legacy tree.
+3. Define the plugin protocol and lifecycle contracts.
+4. Implement configuration, storage, dispatcher, bounded execution, and rate limiting with unit tests.
+5. Implement Telegram connectivity behind an interface and add recorded-update fixtures.
+6. Implement the Python sidecar and compatibility SDK.
+7. Port essential core modules: loader, help, settings, updater, and diagnostics.
+8. Add integration, failure-recovery, race, load, and long-running resource tests.
+9. Document supported legacy APIs, known incompatibilities, and plugin authoring.
+
+At each step, inspect the relevant legacy implementation before coding. Preserve externally observable behavior where practical, but replace unsafe implementation details rather than copying them.
+
+## Required Developer Tooling
+
+Add contributor tooling from the first milestone:
+
+- `gofmt` and `goimports` for Go formatting;
+- `go vet`, `staticcheck`, and `golangci-lint` for syntax and static checks;
+- `go test ./...` and `go test -race ./...`;
+- Ruff formatting and linting for the Python sidecar;
+- `python -m compileall` plus a configured Python type checker;
+- pinned tool versions where practical;
+- repository-local configuration files;
+- pre-commit hooks;
+- CI that runs formatting checks, linting, tests, race tests, and builds on every contribution.
+
+Provide consistent commands through a `Makefile` or equivalent:
+
+```text
+make fmt
+make fmt-check
+make lint
+make test
+make test-race
+make build
+make check
+```
+
+`make check` must run every non-destructive validation required before submitting a contribution. CI and local development must invoke the same commands.
+
+## Quality Gates
+
+- `go build ./...` succeeds.
+- All Go and Python formatting checks pass without modifying files.
+- Static analysis, syntax checks, unit tests, integration tests, and race tests pass.
+- Plugin queues and concurrency are bounded and tested under overload.
+- Plugin crashes, timeouts, malformed RPC messages, and sidecar restarts do not crash the core.
+- Shutdown leaves no workers, subprocesses, sockets, or temporary files behind.
+- Benchmarks report dispatch latency, throughput, allocations, idle RSS, and peak RSS.
+- Golden tests compare selected behavior against recorded legacy cases.
+- Documentation explains setup, architecture, contributing, Go plugin development, Python plugin development, and the compatibility matrix.
+
+## Execution Rules
+
+- Start by inspecting the local sources and writing `serenox-go/docs/architecture.md` and a short milestone checklist.
+- Then immediately scaffold and implement the first buildable vertical slice; do not stop after producing a plan.
+- Prefer small, reviewable changes with tests.
+- Run formatting and all applicable checks after each milestone.
+- Record commands executed and their exact results in the final report.
+- Report only verified progress. Do not mark a feature complete without passing tests.
+- Leave the legacy source tree unchanged and leave the new implementation in `serenox-go/`.
+
+Begin now.
